WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.274
Reading a CV method means identifying the claim, definitions, assumptions and evidence around its equations. Start

00:00:07.274 --> 00:00:14.349
with a small numerical case where you can track every operation and dimension. A familiar symbol

00:00:14.349 --> 00:00:18.770
can hide a normalization or convention the implementation must reproduce.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:25.693
Suppose a method claims to compare feature direction with cosine similarity. First write down the actual

00:00:25.693 --> 00:00:28.090
vectors and verify they are nonzero.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:36.370
Normalization changes magnitude while preserving direction. A paper’s dot-product formula represents cosine only when the required

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:40.740
norms are included or inputs are already unit length.

00:00:41.267 --> 00:00:47.267
Can the raw dot product exceed one while the normalized cosine score remains bounded? Pause here

00:00:47.267 --> 00:00:49.517
and make a prediction before continuing.

00:00:50.633 --> 00:00:57.491
Trace the equation into operations: squared entries, sum, square root, division and dot product. The shape

00:00:57.491 --> 00:01:04.349
of every intermediate should be clear. For our starting inputs, Raw dot product: 3. Vector norm:

00:01:04.349 --> 00:01:06.063
5. Cosine score: 0.6.

00:01:06.567 --> 00:01:12.559
The raw dot product is a different score and can exceed one. An invariant check can

00:01:12.559 --> 00:01:18.177
expose a mismatch between a mathematical claim and its implementation before evaluating the full model.

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:24.860
Defend the connection from equation to code to experiment. State which assumptions were checked and which

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:27.170
claims the evidence does not establish.

